        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed both issues in `json.ts`, but couldn't re-run the linter or my output check: there's no `node_modules` in the folder, and running `node` needed approval I couldn't get.\n\n**The catch:** these objects are serialized to JSON, so sorting their keys alone would change every log line. Lines would go from `{\"message\":…,\"label\":…,\"level\":…}` to `{\"label\":…,\"level\":…,\"message\":…}`, which breaks the rule against changing runtime behavior.\n\n**The fix:**\n- Both object literals (issues 1 and 2, lines 178 and 183) now list their keys in sorted order: `{ label, level, message }`.\n- I added a module constant, `JSON_FIELD_ORDER = [\"message\", \"label\", \"level\"]` (line 161). Both `JSON.stringify` calls now pass it as the second argument in place of `null`.\n\nWhen `JSON.stringify` gets an array there, it writes the properties in the array's order, not the object's order. So the output stays byte-for-byte the same, in both pretty and compact mode.\n\nThat array also acts as a filter, but it can't drop anything here. The object has exactly those three keys, and their values are all strings or `null`, so nothing nested gets filtered out. A `null` label still prints as `\"label\":null`, as before. The `sort-keys` rule doesn't check arrays, so the new constant won't trigger it.",
